ast two years now some of their  members have been tied to plots to bomb  synagogues and nuclear power plants and  they have active members who  currently in the United States military  and none of this really is news right  there was a poll from the military times  a few years ago where 25% of military  members said they encountered white  nationalism in the ranks and that number  is probably higher but I would  understand that there are probably some  people in the military that don't want  to admit that they've encountered white  nationalism in the military so it might  seem sort of obvious that an  organization that lets you carry guns it  might let you go to war maybe you'd get  to kill people that that would attract  violent fascist elements of society so  in a sense it is obvious but it's not  just the military that's at issue it's  really a cultural issue and what I mean  by that is if you look at other  countries yes the military is a job  where you carry guns and maybe you get  to kill people and that's more likely to  appeal to violent fascist elements but  the culture surrounding war and  masculinity and all of these other  issues works as a sort of exacerbating  factor in the United States where you  have a huge contingent that supports  aggressive foreign policy you've got the  problem of our relationship to guns you  have lack of access to mental health  that you have right now a president  whose rhetoric has inflamed this type of  violent ideology so of course you end up  with a military that contains these  extreme elements and my point is even if  the military in some general way is more  likely to attract people predisposed to  violence or to wanting to have the  opportunity to maybe kill people or  shoot people it doesn't actually have to  be as bad as it is in the United States  the military does provide opportunities  to people who might be lacking  opportunities but it also provides  opportunities to people with the  masculinity complex to peo